{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":5000,"raw":false,"start_index":0,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Lula_da_Silva"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Lula_da_Silva:\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Lula da Silva](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Foto_oficial_do_Presidente_da_República_Luiz_Inácio_Lula_da_Silva.jpg \"Lula da Silva\") | |\n|  | |\n| **Second presidency of Lula da Silva** 1 January 2023 – present | |\n| Vice President | [Geraldo Alckmin](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geraldo_Alckmin \"Geraldo Alckmin\") |\n| Cabinet | [*See list*](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_cabinet_of_Lula_da_Silva \"Second cabinet of Lula da Silva\") |\n| Party | [Workers' Party](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Workers'_Party_(Brazil) \"Workers' Party (Brazil)\") |\n| Election | [2022](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Brazilian_general_election \"2022 Brazilian general election\") |\n| [Seat](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seat_of_government \"Seat of government\") | [Palácio do Planalto](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palácio_do_Planalto \"Palácio do Planalto\") (workplace) [Palácio da Alvorada](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palácio_da_Alvorada \"Palácio da Alvorada\") (residence) |\n| ← [Jair Bolsonaro](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Jair_Bolsonaro \"Presidency of Jair Bolsonaro\") | |\n| --- | |\n| [www.lula.com.br](https://www.lula.com.br/) | |\n\nThe **second presidency of Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva** started on 1 January 2023, when he was inaugurated as the 39th [president of Brazil](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Brazil \"President of Brazil\").[[1]](#cite_note-:0-1)[[2]](#cite_note-:2-2) [Lula](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luiz_Inácio_Lula_da_Silva \"Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva\") was elected for a third term as [president of Brazil](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Brazil \"President of Brazil\") on 30 October 2022, by obtaining 50.9% of the valid votes in the [2022 Brazilian general election](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Brazilian_general_election \"2022 Brazilian general election\"), defeating incumbent [Jair Bolsonaro](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jair_Bolsonaro \"Jair Bolsonaro\").[[3]](#cite_note-:3-3) Lula is the first Brazilian president to ever be elected more than twice as well as being the oldest person to ever be elected president in Brazil.[[4]](#cite_note-:5-4)\n\nOn 20 May 2021, in an interview with French magazine *[Paris Match](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris_Match \"Paris Match\")*, Lula confirmed that he was a pre-candidate for the next year's elections.[[5]](#cite_note-:6-5)[[6]](#cite_note-:7-6)\n\nThe [Workers' Party](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Workers'_Party_(Brazil) \"Workers' Party (Brazil)\") national committee, on 13 April 2022, approved the nomination of former Governor of [São Paulo](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/São_Paulo_(state) \"São Paulo (state)\") [Geraldo Alckmin](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geraldo_Alckmin \"Geraldo Alckmin\") (PSB) for vice president.[[7]](#cite_note-7) The ticket was officialized on 7 May, in a coalition formed by the [Brazil of Hope Federation](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazil_of_Hope \"Brazil of Hope\") (formed by [Workers' Party](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Workers'_Party_(Brazil) \"Workers' Party (Brazil)\"), [Communist Party of Brazil](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communist_Party_of_Brazil \"Communist Party of Brazil\") and [Green Party](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Party_(Brazil) \"Green Party (Brazil)\")), [Brazilian Socialist Party](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazilian_Socialist_Party \"Brazilian Socialist Party\"), [Solidarity](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solidarity_(Brazil) \"Solidarity (Brazil)\") and [PSOL REDE Federation](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PSOL_REDE_Federation \"PSOL REDE Federation\").[[8]](#cite_note-:8-8)[[9]](#cite_note-9) With the withdrawal of [André Janones](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/André_Janones \"André Janones\") on 4 August, the ticket received official support of [Avante](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avante_(political_party) \"Avante (political party)\") and [Agir](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Act_(Brazil) \"Act (Brazil)\").[[10]](#cite_note-:9-10)\n\nThe campaign was marked by [polarization](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_polarization \"Political polarization\") and [Manichaeism](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manichaeism_(philosophy)?action=edit&redlink=1 \"Manichaeism (philosophy) (page does not exist)\"), in which Lula's campaign tried to characterize him as the only alternative in the [second round](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Two-round_system \"Two-round system\") for those who believed in [democracy](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democracy \"Democracy\") and [human rights](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_rights \"Human rights\"), while Bolsonaro sought to associate Lula with [communism](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communism \"Communism\")/[socialism](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Socialism \"Socialism\") and [far-left](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Far-left_politics \"Far-left politics\") [anti-democratic regimes](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dictatorship \"Dictatorship\") such as [Venezuela](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venezuela \"Venezuela\"),[[11]](#cite_note-11) [Cuba](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuba \"Cuba\"),[[12]](#cite_note-12) [Bolivia](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bolivia \"Bolivia\"),[[13]](#cite_note-13) and [Nicaragua](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicaragua \"Nicaragua\").[[14]](#cite_note-14) The new governme\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 5000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
