{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"agent_message","text":"The web_search tool is now consistently returning errors. I have, however, gathered a substantial body of new material to expand the dossier. Let me also try to write the material — the parent task said \"If writing either is unavailable, continue the research and put the complete handoff in the final response for parent persistence\" — the previous attempt failed because the path was outside the sandbox. The handoff for round 1 is now in the final response below.\n\n---\n\n# Round 1 evidence-collection handoff — Brazil — Lula — 2023\n\n## Scope and method\nResumed evidence collection after reviewer round 1. Worked through reviewer-flagged gaps: 1B (nuclear/existential), 2B (international conduct), 5B (independent outcomes), 6B (programme/delivery), 7B (personal integrity), 8B (effectiveness). Did not re-fetch supplied local structured priors; did not use the client matrix. Did not assign scores. Local file writes remained unavailable (sandbox blocked); full handoff is delivered in this response.\n\n## Source families added this round\n- ICAN (TPNW tracker)\n- Associação Brasileira de Imprensa — APIA / Reach Critical Will (Brazil TPNW statements)\n- Bull. Atomic Scientists (Brazil-IAEA nuclear submarine negotiations)\n- NTI (TPNW treaty profile)\n- Covington & Burling (Brazil public-policy practice)\n- AP News (Brazil lower-house tax reform vote)\n- Trench Rossi (Brazil tax reform approval)\n- Le Monde (Yanomami emergency)\n- Mongabay (PRODES deforestation)\n- Wikipedia (second presidency, 2022–2023 election protests, blocking of X)\n\n## New evidence items\n\n### E022 — Brazil TPNW statement at G7 Hiroshima\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://www.icanw.org/brazil_and_indonesia_likely_to_ratify_tpnw_soon|Lula at G7 Hiroshima|nuclear disarmament statement May 2023`\n- **URL:** https://www.icanw.org/brazil_and_indonesia_likely_to_ratify_tpnw_soon\n- **Publisher/date:** International Campaign to Abolish Nuclear Weapons (ICAN), 7 July 2023\n- **Locator/excerpt:** \"Brazil's new president, Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva, said during a G7 summit in Hiroshima, Japan, in May 2023: 'As long as nuclear weapons exist, there will always be the possibility of their use. It was for this reason that Brazil was actively engaged in the negotiations of the [TPNW], which we hope to be able to ratify soon.'\" ICAN also reports: \"The foreign affairs and national defence committee of the chamber of deputies – the lower house of the federal congress – is currently preparing a report on the TPNW, after which it will be sent to the federal senate for final review.\"\n- **Source profile:** Civil-society coalition (ICAN) with high credibility on TPNW-specific reporting; reports direct attribution to Lula plus concrete legislative pipeline.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023; ruler attribution: high for the quoted statement; the parliamentary report is institutional and may continue past 2023.\n- **Role:** Direct positive 1B evidence (nuclear risk reduction, disarmament advocacy, treaty support) and direct 2B evidence (peace and law-promoting diplomacy).\n- **Maps:** 1B.1, 1B.2, 1B.4, 1B.8, 2B.1, 2B.7.\n\n### E023 — Brazil-IAEA naval propulsion safeguards negotiation\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://www.iaea.org/sites/default/files/documents/govinf2023-11.pdf|Section on Brazil|Brazil 2023 naval propulsion safeguards proposal`\n- **URL:** https://www.iaea.org/sites/default/files/documents/govinf2023-11.pdf\n- **Publisher/date:** IAEA, 31 May 2023 (Board of Governors information document)\n- **Locator:** PDF contains a 2023-11 reference and was indexed as May 2023 in Brave snippets. The exact article and paragraph were not opened in raw PDF form; the document is the 2023 Board information series on naval nuclear propulsion.\n- **Source profile:** Intergovernmental primary record (IAEA); source-confidence high for direct factual statements about the safeguards proposal.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023 Board series; ruler attribution: the safeguards proposal reflects ongoing federal policy, not a fresh 2023 single decision.\n- **Role:** Mixed/contextual 1B evidence — Brazil was negotiating special procedures for naval-propagation nuclear material with the IAEA and ABACC, a non-proliferation and safeguards topic.\n- **Note:** The PDF was not successfully decoded in the worker's fetch; this item is based on the discovery snippet and the IAEA URL. It is a recoverable lead but should be re-extracted with a proper PDF parser before being treated as full final evidence. Disposition: `context`; **locator: missing PDF page**.\n- **Maps:** 1B.3, 1B.4.\n\n### E024 — Covington first-six-months economic-policy assessment\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://www.cov.com/en/news-and-insights/insights/2023/07/brazils-lula-first-six-months-wins-losses-and-missed-opportunities-in-economic-policy|First six months|Wins losses missed opportunities`\n- **URL:** https://www.cov.com/en/news-and-insights/insights/2023/07/brazils-lula-first-six-months-wins-losses-and-missed-opportunities-in-economic-policy\n- **Publisher/date:** Covington & Burling Public Policy practice, 25 July 2023\n- **Locator:** HTML body, sections \"Economic Policy Priorities,\" \"Policy Wins,\" \"Policy Losses,\" \"Missed Policy Opportunities.\"\n- **Claim:** Reports Lula and Finance Minister Haddad's three-track economic policy (fiscal framework, tax revenue, consumption-tax reform) and the 6-month record: the new fiscal framework bill was introduced in the House of Deputies on 18 April, approved (with amendments) on 23 May with 372 of 513 votes, approved by the Senate on 21 June with 57 of 81; a \"historic tax reform\" was approved by the House on 7 July, 382 votes in round one and 375 in round two; the National Monetary Council kept 2023–2025 inflation targets and set a 2026 target of 3.00% in continuous-targeting form. Of Lula's first 10 provisional measures, only 2 were converted into law and 8 expired (including the tax-revenue measures). Lula also issued MPs increasing spending and tax incentives for fuels and vehicles, which ran counter to Haddad's spending-control goal. The administration lost an attempt to reverse the new waste/water sector legal framework.\n- **Source profile:** Law-firm public-policy analysis; medium-high confidence for legislative process facts, medium for evaluations; some material is corporate-affiliated and is opinion-influenced.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023 (first six months).\n- **Attribution:** Strong for the legislative actions and the executive's stated goals; mixed for the failures, where the description is structural.\n- **Role:** Strong positive 5B/8B evidence on the fiscal framework operationalization and the tax-reform passage; strong negative 5B/8B evidence on revenue-raising failures, MP losses, and contradictory spending moves.\n- **Maps:** 5B.1, 5B.2, 5B.3, 5B.4, 5B.6, 5B.7, 5B.8, 5B.9, 7B.8 (promises vs implementation), 8B.1, 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.8, 8B.9, 8B.10.\n\n### E025 — Covington fiscal-framework final-approval analysis\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://www.cov.com/en/news-and-insights/insights/2023/08/brazils-new-fiscal-framework-approved-by-congress|Framework mechanics|August 22 final vote 379 to 64`\n- **URL:** https://www.cov.com/en/news-and-insights/insights/2023/08/brazils-new-fiscal-framework-approved-by-congress\n- **Publisher/date:** Covington & Burling Public Policy practice, August 2023\n- **Locator:** HTML body sections \"Framework mechanics\" and \"Reliance on revenue increase.\"\n- **Claim:** On 22 August 2023, the House of Deputies held a final vote on the new fiscal framework, approving it 379 to 64. The framework establishes a primary budget surplus target from -0.5% of GDP in 2023 to +1.0% in 2026, in 0.5 pp annual steps, within a ±0.25 pp tolerance band. Spending growth is pegged to 70% of real revenue growth (50% if target is missed), with a 0.6% floor and a 2.5% ceiling. Congress added cost-reduction provisions, mandatory fiscal adjustment in non-compliance, and limits on investment-financed additional spending. The administration framed the design as counter-cyclical; critics view it as overreliant on revenue increases rather than spending cuts. Described as \"the first major legislative victory of the administration.\"\n- **Source profile:** Law-firm public-policy analysis; medium-high confidence; corporate-affiliated perspective.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Attribution:** Direct to Lula's proposed framework; Congress's adjustments are institutional.\n- **Role:** 5B/8B implementation evidence with mixed evaluation.\n- **Maps:** 5B.1, 5B.3, 5B.7, 5B.8, 5B.9, 5B.10, 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.8, 8B.10.\n\n### E026 — Trench Rossi tax-reform final-approval analysis\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://www.trenchrossi.com/en/legal-alerts/brazil-tax-reform-approved-by-the-congress/|summary|House approval Dec 16 2023`\n- **URL:** https://www.trenchrossi.com/en/legal-alerts/brazil-tax-reform-approved-by-the-congress/\n- **Publisher/date:** Trench Rossi Watanabe, 19 December 2023\n- **Locator:** \"In summary\" section of the legal alert.\n- **Claim:** On 16 December 2023, the House of Representatives approved the tax-reform bill (PEC 45) in its final round after the Senate had already approved. The Senate-included items that the House excluded from the final version include: a \"Sin Tax\" (IS) and CIDE; the Federative Council/Management Committee design; a multi-year IBS distribution rule; ZFM benefits; reduced-rate sectors; mandatory economic studies for tax changes; joint IBS/CBS regulation; and a hydrogen provision. The next step is supplementary laws to be discussed in the first half of 2024; the Executive must within 90 days send income-tax and payroll-tax reform bills.\n- **Source profile:** Law-firm tax analysis; medium-high confidence; corporate perspective.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Attribution:** Lula's executive role is in the submission and signing; Congress held the final vote; the Executive retains follow-on commitments.\n- **Role:** Strong 5B/8B evidence on the tax-reform passage and its contested shape.\n- **Maps:** 5B.1, 5B.2, 5B.3, 5B.4, 5B.5, 5B.7, 5B.8, 5B.10, 7B.8, 8B.1, 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.8, 8B.9, 8B.10.\n\n### E027 — AP News tax-reform House approval\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://apnews.com/article/brazil-congress-tax-reform-lula-d2cccbd2d586ea5a7de60891a6ff1e33|first round July 7 2023|Brazil lower house 382 and 370 votes`\n- **URL:** https://apnews.com/article/brazil-congress-tax-reform-lula-d2cccbd2d586ea5a7de60891a6ff1e33\n- **Publisher/date:** Associated Press, 7 July 2023\n- **Locator:** Body text: \"Brazil's lower house of Congress approved a major reform of the nation… early Friday,\" \"the constitutional amendment required a 60% majority in two rounds of voting before proceeding to the Senate. It passed easily by greater than 3-to-1 margins in both votes.\"\n- **Claim:** The Brazilian lower house approved the constitutional amendment to merge five consumption taxes (IPI, COFINS, PIS, ICMS, ISS) into two value-added taxes (federal CBS and subnational IBS). Speaker Arthur Lira, a Bolsonaro ally, called for cross-party support; São Paulo governor Tarcísio de Freitas negotiated with Haddad before the vote. Lula tweeted: \"Brazil will have its first tax reform of the democratic period. A historic moment and a great victory for the country.\" The reform's progress followed record allocations to lawmakers' state projects, illustrating government reliance on earmarks.\n- **Source profile:** International wire service; high confidence.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Attribution:** Lula's quoted statement; the legislative coalition is institutional.\n- **Role:** Strong 5B/8B/7B evidence.\n- **Maps:** 5B.1, 5B.3, 5B.4, 5B.5, 5B.6, 5B.8, 5B.10, 7B.8, 8B.1, 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.8, 8B.9.\n\n### E028 — Covington tax-reform Senate-process analysis\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://www.cov.com/en/news-and-insights/insights/2023/11/key-vote-on-tax-reform-expected-in-brazils-senate|Pressure politics|Key changes by rapporteur`\n- **URL:** https://www.cov.com/en/news-and-insights/insights/2023/11/key-vote-on-tax-reform-expected-in-brazils-senate\n- **Publisher/date:** Covington & Burling, 7 November 2023\n- **Locator:** Body section \"Pressure Politics in the Senate\" and \"Key Changes by the Rapporteur.\"\n- **Claim:** Describes the November 2023 Senate pre-vote landscape and the rapporteur's key changes (VAT rate ceiling/neutrality mechanism; more exceptions and a 30% rate band; stricter Congressional control of the Selective Tax; increased federal funding to states; transformation of the Federative Council into a Management Committee without normative power, with a president to be approved by the Senate). The administration's strategy is to raise government revenue to hit the fiscal-framework targets; private-sector and state groups press for exceptions and compensation.\n- **Source profile:** Law-firm tax/policy analysis; medium-high confidence; corporate perspective.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Attribution:** Lula's administration is the proponent; rapporteur is institutional.\n- **Role:** Strong 5B/8B evidence on contested policy design and coalition bargaining.\n- **Maps:** 5B.1, 5B.2, 5B.3, 5B.4, 5B.5, 5B.7, 5B.8, 5B.9, 5B.10, 7B.8, 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.8, 8B.9, 8B.10.\n\n### E029 — Le Monde Yanomami emergency coverage\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2023/01/22/brazil-lula-declares-public-health-emergency-for-yanomami-people_6012537_4.html|Boa Vista Jan 21 2023|Lula visit and emergency decree`\n- **URL:** https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2023/01/22/brazil-lula-declares-public-health-emergency-for-yanomami-people_6012537_4.html\n- **Publisher/date:** Le Monde (with AP), 22 January 2023\n- **Locator:** Body sections \"Yanomami people,\" \"Illegal mining,\" \"Bolsonaro government.\"\n- **Claim:** Lula visited Roraima on 21 January 2023 after Health Minister Nisia Trindade signed a public-health emergency decree for the Yanomami people. The decree has no expiration date, allows extra personnel, and requires regular reports. Lula created a multiministerial committee coordinated by his chief of staff for 90 days. The Yanomami live in the largest Indigenous area in Brazil (more than 9 million hectares, population around 30,000). Under Bolsonaro, the number of health personnel decreased and four health units were shut down. Between 2019 and 2022, 570 Yanomami children died from curable diseases, per the cited Sumaúma report; the prior Bolsonaro period saw a 29% jump in deaths of children aged 5 or less compared to the previous government.\n- **Source profile:** International newspaper; high confidence; AP corroboration.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Attribution:** Strong for the decree, committee, and visit; inherited mortality and structural conditions are not Lula-caused.\n- **Role:** Strong 3B/6B/8B evidence for the federal emergency response; strong 3B/6B contrary evidence for the inherited crisis.\n- **Maps:** 3B.4, 3B.6, 3B.7, 3B.9, 3B.10, 4B.7, 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.6, 6B.8, 6B.9, 6B.10, 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.8, 8B.10.\n\n### E030 — Mongabay PRODES 2023 deforestation\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://news.mongabay.com/2023/11/deforestation-in-the-brazilian-amazon-falls-22-in-2023/|22% decrease|Nov 9 2023 PRODES data`\n- **URL:** https://news.mongabay.com/2023/11/deforestation-in-the-brazilian-amazon-falls-22-in-2023/\n- **Publisher/date:** Mongabay, 9 November 2023\n- **Locator:** Body and headline.\n- **Claim:** Deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon decreased by 22% in the year ending 31 July 2023, according to PRODES data released by Brazil's National Institute for Space Research (INPE). (Note: this is the Aug 2022–Jul 2023 PRODES year, which overlaps Lula's first seven months.)\n- **Source profile:** Environmental journalism using official INPE data; medium-high confidence.\n- **Temporal fit:** PRODES year spanning 2022–2023; the 22% decrease figure is a transitional change partially attributable to enforcement shifts under Lula, but most of the period precedes his term and the change is measured against a pre-Lula baseline.\n- **Attribution:** Mixed; partial to Lula's early-month enforcement actions, partial inherited.\n- **Role:** Strong contextual 1B.9 / 3B.7 / 5B.6 / 6B.6 evidence; partial Lula attribution.\n- **Maps:** 1B.9 (broad context only), 3B.7, 5B.6, 5B.7, 6B.6, 6B.8, 8B.7, 8B.8.\n\n### E031 — Wikipedia second presidency page (orienting reference)\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Lula_da_Silva|Article opening|Second presidency start, coalition, inauguration`\n- **URL:** https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Lula_da_Silva\n- **Publisher/date:** Wikipedia, accessed 2026-07-17\n- **Locator:** Article body.\n- **Claim:** Lula's second presidency began 1 January 2023 as the 39th president; vice-president Geraldo Alckmin; Workers' Party-led Brazil of Hope Federation coalition with PSB, Solidarity, and the PSOL REDE Federation. The 2022 election: Lula won 50.9% of valid votes on 30 October 2022.\n- **Source profile:** Encyclopedia; medium-high confidence; recoverable orientation; specific numbers and dates can be cross-referenced.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Attribution:** High for the inauguration; campaign context predates 2023.\n- **Role:** Orientation/context.\n- **Maps:** 4B.1, 4B.4, 8B.1.\n\n### E032 — Wikipedia 2022–2023 Brazilian election protests\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022%E2%80%932023_Brazilian_election_protests|Background|2022-2023 election protest timeline`\n- **URL:** https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022%E2%80%932023_Brazilian_election_protests\n- **Publisher/date:** Wikipedia, accessed 2026-07-17\n- **Locator:** Article body, \"Background,\" \"Result,\" and \"Methods\" sections.\n- **Claim:** The 2022–2023 Brazilian election protests began after 30 October 2022 and culminated in the 8 January 2023 attack on Praça dos Três Poderes. The protests blocked roads, set up camps outside military buildings, and sought to overturn the election. Congress, the Supreme Court, and the Electoral Court certified Lula's victory. After the 8 January attack, mass arrests followed and the 8 January CPMI was established. Bolsonaro ultimately acknowledged defeat and condemned the 8 January attacks. Small-scale protests continued into the Lula presidency.\n- **Source profile:** Encyclopedia; medium-high confidence; recoverable orientation.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023 for the conclusion and aftermath.\n- **Attribution:** Context; the article is about Bolsonaro-supporting protests, not Lula's actions, but it bounds the institutional environment.\n- **Role:** 4B/3B context for the inherited situation and Lula's first-month institutional defense.\n- **Maps:** 3B.9, 4B.4, 4B.10, 8B.5.\n\n### E033 — BCB Open Letter to CMN (Lula-friction context)\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://bcb.gov.br/en/pressdetail/2461/nota|Campos Neto open letter|Open letter and tension with government`\n- **URL:** https://bcb.gov.br/en/pressdetail/2461/nota\n- **Publisher/date:** Banco Central do Brasil, 1 October 2023\n- **Locator:** Body and title \"Open Letter to CMN's president.\"\n- **Claim:** The BCB released the Open Letter from BCB Governor Roberto Campos Neto to the President of the National Monetary Council, Finance Minister Fernando Haddad. This is in the public record as a 2023 institutional tension between the central bank and the Finance Ministry.\n- **Source profile:** Official primary record (BCB); high confidence.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Attribution:** Mixed; central-bank autonomy friction is a known and Lula-relevant context; the open letter's specific content could not be re-extracted in this session.\n- **Role:** 5B/7B/8B context for central-bank tension; only at recovery-stage depth; specific claim text should be re-extracted.\n- **Maps:** 5B.3, 5B.7, 7B.1, 7B.2, 7B.8, 8B.7, 8B.8.\n\n### E034 — BCB Governor-of-the-Year profile (Campos Neto)\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://latinfinance.com/magazine/2023-q4/2023/10/05/central-bank-governor-of-the-year-roberto-campos-neto/|Recognition and criticism|2023 central-bank governance`\n- **URL:** https://latinfinance.com/magazine/2023-q4/2023/10/05/central-bank-governor-of-the-year-roberto-campos-neto/\n- **Publisher/date:** LatinFinance, 5 October 2023\n- **Locator:** Body.\n- **Claim:** Roberto Campos Neto was named 2023 Central Bank Governor of the Year by LatinFinance. He was appointed in 2019, under Bolsonaro, and continued in office in 2023 under Lula. The relationship with the Lula government was publicly strained, with the BCB Open Letter (E033) and critical remarks by Lula and allies.\n- **Source profile:** Trade publication; medium-high confidence; industry-affiliated perspective.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023.\n- **Attribution:** Indirect; documents the relationship and 2023 friction.\n- **Role:** 5B/7B/8B context for central-bank relationship.\n- **Maps:** 5B.3, 5B.7, 7B.1, 7B.2, 7B.6, 7B.8, 8B.5, 8B.7, 8B.8.\n\n### E035 — ICAN Brazil country page\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://www.icanw.org/brazil|Profile|Signed not ratified TPNW`\n- **URL:** https://www.icanw.org/brazil\n- **Publisher/date:** ICAN, page accessed 2026-07-17\n- **Locator:** Body.\n- **Claim:** Brazil has signed but not yet ratified the TPNW. The page notes that in 2023, Lula, \"a strong supporter of the TPNW,\" had pledged to pursue ratification. The page lists Brazil as among the treaty signatories.\n- **Source profile:** Civil-society coalition; high confidence on TPNW-specific procedural facts.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023 (statement of position).\n- **Attribution:** Medium (Lula's stated support; ratification requires Congress).\n- **Role:** 1B.1/1B.4/2B.7/7B.1/7B.8 supporting context.\n- **Maps:** 1B.1, 1B.4, 2B.7, 7B.1, 7B.8.\n\n### E036 — Wikipedia \"Blocking of X\" page (context)\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blocking_of_X_in_Brazil|Background|2024 STF Moraes order, not 2023`\n- **URL:** https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blocking_of_X_in_Brazil\n- **Publisher/date:** Wikipedia, accessed 2026-07-17\n- **Locator:** Article opening.\n- **Claim:** Justice Alexandre de Moraes unilaterally imposed a block of the social-networking site X (formerly Twitter) in Brazil. The order to block is generally dated to August 2024, not 2023; the page describes the order as \"complete block of access to X throughout Brazil\" on 30 August 2024.\n- **Source profile:** Encyclopedia; medium-high confidence; 2024-dated.\n- **Temporal fit:** Post-2023; not a 2023 evidence item.\n- **Attribution:** Not Lula's direct action; STF Justice Moraes' order.\n- **Role:** Important boundary marker — Lula's administration in 2023 *unblocked* >3,000 users from institutional accounts; the 2024 STF order blocking X is not a 2023 action and should not be attributed to Lula in 2023.\n- **Maps:** None in 2023.\n\n### E037 — Reuters-reported Amazon deforestation 50% decrease for 2023\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/deforestation-brazils-amazon-down-by-50-five-year-low-2023-2024-01-12/|50% decrease|Jan 12 2024 PRODES release for 2023 calendar year`\n- **URL:** https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/deforestation-brazils-amazon-down-by-50-five-year-low-2023-2024-01-12/\n- **Publisher/date:** Reuters, 12 January 2024 (reporting 2023 PRODES data released in early 2024)\n- **Locator:** Headline and standard Reuters wire layout.\n- **Claim:** Brazil's Amazon deforestation in the calendar year 2023 was half the previous year's level and the lowest since 2018, per government data released on 12 January 2024.\n- **Source profile:** Major international wire; high confidence for the headline figure; the underlying page is the Reuters URL (content not opened due to robots).\n- **Temporal fit:** 2023 calendar year; ruler attribution: 2023 enforcement policy is partly attributable to Lula, but the percentage change also reflects the comparison base.\n- **Role:** Direct 5B/6B/8B outcome evidence; strong positive for environment-related outcomes.\n- **Maps:** 5B.6, 5B.7, 5B.10, 6B.6, 6B.8, 6B.10, 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.8, 8B.10.\n\n### E038 — Covington public-policy practice additional context\n- **canonical_fact_key:** `https://www.cov.com/en/news-and-insights/insights/2023/06/key-vote-expected-on-brazils-historic-tax-reform|Detail|VAT design consolidation and regional development`\n- **URL:** https://www.cov.com/en/news-and-insights/insights/2023/06/key-vote-expected-on-brazils-historic-tax-reform\n- **Publisher/date:** Covington & Burling, June 2023\n- **Locator:** Body sections \"Consumption Taxes Changes\" and \"Property Taxes Changes.\"\n- **Claim:** The reform merges IPI, COFINS and PIS into a federal CBS, and ICMS and ISS into a subnational IBS; creates a new Selective Tax on health/environmental externalities; adds IPVA to private jets and vessels; makes ITCMD progressive and extends it to overseas assets; streamlines IPTU rate increases. VAT phasing: 2026–2033; transition to destination-based charging: 2029–2078 (50 years). Tax evasion estimated USD 100 billion, tax disputes USD 400 billion; reform expected to add USD 240 billion to Brazil's GDP over 15 years.\n- **Source profile:** Law-firm public-policy analysis; medium-high confidence.\n- **Temporal fit:** Exact 2023 reform design.\n- **Attribution:** Strong for the administration's design; Congress adopted with modifications.\n- **Role:** 5B/8B detailed design evidence.\n- **Maps:** 5B.1, 5B.3, 5B.4, 5B.5, 5B.6, 5B.8, 5B.10, 8B.1, 8B.2, 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.8.\n\n### E039 — Mongabay/Mongabay-style 22% PRODES decrease (E030) — re-mapped to the full calendar year via Reuters E037\n- These two items together bracket the deforestation decrease: PRODES year ending 31 July 2023 (E030) shows a 22% decrease; full calendar year 2023 (E037) shows a 50% decrease. Both are official INPE outputs.\n\n## Chapter-by-chapter update (round 1)\n\n### 1B — Nuclear and existential-risk responsibility\n\n- **New evidence:** E022 (Lula's G7 Hiroshima statement), E023 (Brazil-IAEA naval-propagation safeguards negotiations; only context), E035 (ICAN profile of TPNW support).\n- **Improvement:** E022 is a direct, quoted, ruler-attributed statement on TPNW and nuclear disarmament, with a high-confidence source; the TPNW pipeline is concrete and traceable. This is the strongest 1B evidence captured in the dossier and supports 1B.1, 1B.2, 1B.4, 1B.8.\n- **Status of chapter:** E022 provides at least one direct, target-year, ruler-attributed item. Combined with E023 (IAEA negotiations) and E035 (TPNW profile), the chapter has moved from zero defensible direct evidence to a small but traceable core.\n- **Remaining gaps:** Brazilian nuclear-submarine / naval-propagation command safety; dual-use/cyber/biological/AI governance; Brazil-specific 2023 crises; broad institutional details on command and safety.\n\n### 2B — International peace, restraint, and lawful security\n\n- **New evidence:** E022 (Hiroshima statement and TPNW-ratification push), E029 (federal response to a domestic humanitarian crisis, contextual for international conduct).\n- **Improvement:** E022 strengthens 2B.1 and 2B.7 (peace-and-law diplomacy, mediation). E009 already covers Ukraine/Venezuela/Nicaragua/China/Gaza positions.\n- **Remaining gaps:** Brazilian arms exports in 2023 (SIPRI 2023 fact-sheet data exists but the PDF was not decoded in this session); concrete peace-process outcomes; military conduct abroad; UCDP/PA-X narrative; military-accountability proceedings. The 2023 PRODES/Reuters environment numbers are not international-peace material.\n\n### 3B — Domestic safety, r